On 17.10.2012 16:14, Jonadab the Unsightly One wrote:
> On Oct 16, 11:22 pm, Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanag...@hotmail.com>
> wrote:
>> This sounds overly complicated. Why not, instead of those
>> eight individual steps, just memorise everything that is on
>> the square, where the player (and maybe also the killer) is,
>> and link those data into a fresh Sokoban level of the same
>> type?
> 
> Heh.  I suppose that would also work.  It leads to a
> somewhat more predictable bones level (e.g., you
> know random items you find elsewhere on the level
> have not been tampered with by the former player),

Well, not necessarily. The primary issues in Sokoban
that require close attention are the boulders and pits.
All other items, the guaranteed scrolls on entry level,
or the wand, ring, and food on all levels need not be
set up to the original (non-bones) status. Critical may
be an additional boulder object on the players square,
but that can be handled easily.

All that requires special treatment in the code, though,
which I think should be avoided if there's not a concrete
interesting game play goal associated with it.

Another option could be to rewrite the bones logic in a
more general way, so that all those non-bones special
levels could be handled uniformly together with all the
other levels. But that requires a thorough examination
of the problem structure in the given cases and more
effort.

> but that is arguably not a very big problem, if it
> simplifies the code significantly, which it might.
> 
>> BTW, I don't see any game play justification for that effort.
> 
> What's the game-play justification for scoreboards?

Scoreboards? - what Nethack feature are you referring to?

> 
> Saving and loading bones is not so much about direct
> impact on gameplay itself as it is about social aspects
> of the game as a franchise. 

Sure. My point is another one; Sokoban is a special level
that already requires special rules and special code to
handle all that. Excluding that Sokoban branch from bones
generation will not take the (IMO very nice) bones concept
from the game.

> NetHack without bones is
> entirely a single-player game (unless you have people
> kibitzing, either via watch/mail or simply by standing
> over your shoulder).  Finding evidence of the efforts
> and failed attempts of other players, and knowing that
> someone else _might_ find evidence of your game,
> is inherently interesting to some players.  It's no less
> interesting for Sokoban than for any other level. 

Yes, bones are interesting as concept, but it's unnecessary
to extend that to every level, and specifically not in the
given case where you would need yet another special handling,
and all that for just a small branch like Sokoban.

> I'm
> pretty sure the reason Sokoban is traditionally not
> eligible for bones is because of the risk of negative
> impact on gameplay.  If that risk could be effectively
> mitigated, then it would be as worthwhile to save
> bones for Sokoban as for any other level.

I think the Devteam wanted to avoid inconsistencies and hard
to handle or hidden problems in that special branch. But who
knows...

Janis



